{% comment %}
  sections/home-shop-grid.liquid
  Ported from the "Arcane Flame Website Prototype" option-c.html:
    - CSS: lines ~299-323 (.pgrid, .pcard, .pcard__img, .pcard__tag,
      .pcard__body + children, .attrib*) plus the .pgrid/.attrib-relevant
      slice of the <=1080px and <=680px responsive rules — see
      shopify-theme/assets/home-shop-grid.css for the full colour-
      substitution notes.
    - Markup: lines ~655-701 (<section class="sec sec--mid" id="shop">
      ...</section>) — the "Pre-printed miniatures." product grid and the
      DM Stash attribution lockup immediately below it.

  ** Per the user's explicit decision on 2026-08-30, this section departs
  from the source in one deliberate way: the source hardcodes 4 fictional
  products with fake "From £X.00" prices. That is unacceptable on a live
  commerce page, so this section pulls real Shopify product data from a
  merchant-assigned collection instead. Nothing about product identity,
  imagery or pricing is hardcoded here. **

  Schema/rendering decisions:

  1. `collection` (type "collection") + `products_to_show` (type "range",
     1-12, default 4 — matching the source's 4 cards) drive the grid.
     Guarded on the actual card count (`shown`), not merely on whether a
     collection is assigned: a fresh section placement has no collection
     assigned at all, and a collection can also be assigned but currently
     hold zero products (e.g. a merchant empties/unpublishes it) — both
     fall through to the same "Select a collection..." empty state (new —
     the source never had either case, since its products were hardcoded
     and always present) rather than a broken/empty grid. See
     .pgrid--empty in home-shop-grid.css. (Fixed post-review 2026-08-30 —
     the first version only checked `section.settings.collection`, which
     covered the no-collection case but left an assigned-but-empty
     collection rendering a bare `<div class="pgrid"></div>`.)

  2. Card fields map to real Shopify product data:
       - image  -> product.featured_image | image_url (not the source's
         hardcoded assets/img/*.png paths)
       - title  -> product.title
       - price  -> product.price | money (the store's real, formatted
         money value — not the source's hardcoded "From £X.00" string)
       - "scale" line (source: "32mm heroic · resin" / "Large format ·
         resin" / "Playable terrain · PLA") -> product.type. This is a
         judgment call: product.type is a real, built-in Shopify field
         every product already has (no new tagging/metafield scheme
         needs to be invented or documented for merchants to fill in),
         and it plays the same "what kind of thing is this" role the
         source's hand-written scale/material line did. It won't read
         identically to the source's specific "32mm heroic · resin"
         phrasing — that's expected, since real products won't share the
         prototype's fictional taxonomy. Hidden entirely when blank.

  3. `.pcard__tag` ("Bestseller" / "Terrain", shown on 2 of the source's
     4 hardcoded cards) is DROPPED for this collection-driven grid rather
     than kept. The task brief explicitly warns against inventing
     product-tag/metafield logic that doesn't exist yet, and there is no
     existing Shopify field that maps onto "which products get a
     highlight ribbon" today (unlike product.type above, which already
     exists on every product). Tying it to product.tags contains
     'bestseller' would require merchants to adopt a specific tagging
     convention this task has no mandate to define. If a merchant wants
     this back later, the natural home is a real signal that already
     exists (e.g. a collection sort/tag convention agreed with the
     merchant) — flagged for a future task rather than guessed at here.

  4. The DM Stash attribution lockup (.attrib*) is HARDCODED verbatim,
     not editable — same rule Task 9 (home-services.liquid) applied to
     its AttributionLockup badge, and consistent with the "legally-
     reviewed licensing wording" flagged on home-footer.liquid's separate
     (editable, ordinary-copy) DM Stash line. This one is a fixed
     licensing-relationship statement co-located with real product
     pricing, so — like the services badge — it stays out of merchant
     control entirely: no section/block setting exposes its text.
     Its "Browse the full range" link is real, not the source's `href="#"`
     placeholder: it points at section.settings.collection.url when a
     collection is assigned. My call: the link is omitted entirely (not
     rendered) when no collection is set, rather than falling back to
     `#` — a dead link next to an empty-state grid would be confusing,
     and this section already has a clear empty-state message covering
     the no-collection case.

  5. Judgment call — dm-stash.png: like arcane-flame-white.png in
     home-nav.liquid / home-hero.liquid / home-footer.liquid, this
     references an asset filename that does not yet exist in
     shopify-theme/assets/. Kept the same asset_url precedent (this is a
     fixed licensing lockup, not a merchant-editable image_picker) rather
     than inventing a workaround; tracked as the same known asset gap.
{% endcomment %}
<section class="sec sec--mid" id="Shop-{{ section.id }}">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec__head">
      <p class="eyebrow reveal">{{ section.settings.eyebrow }}</p>
      <h2 class="display reveal" data-d="1">{{ section.settings.heading }}</h2>
      <div class="lede reveal" data-d="2">{{ section.settings.lede }}</div>
    </div>

    {% comment %}
      shown/product_cards computed before deciding which wrapper to render,
      so a collection assigned but currently empty (0 products — e.g. a
      merchant un-publishes or clears every product in it) falls through to
      the same empty-state message as no-collection-assigned, instead of a
      bare empty <div class="pgrid"></div> that reads as broken. Fixed
      post-review 2026-08-30 — see task-12-report.md.
    {% endcomment %}
    {% assign shown = 0 %}
    {% if section.settings.collection %}
      {% assign shop_collection = section.settings.collection %}
      {% capture product_cards %}
        {% for product in shop_collection.products %}
          {% if shown >= section.settings.products_to_show %}
            {% break %}
          {% endif %}
          <article class="pcard reveal" {% if forloop.index0 > 0 %}data-d="{{ forloop.index0 }}"{% endif %}>
            <div class="pcard__img">
              {% if product.featured_image %}
                <img
                  src="{{ product.featured_image | image_url: width: 600 }}"
                  srcset="{{ product.featured_image | image_url: width: 300 }} 300w, {{ product.featured_image | image_url: width: 600 }} 600w"
                  alt="{{ product.featured_image.alt | default: product.title }}"
                  loading="lazy"
                  width="{{ product.featured_image.width }}"
                  height="{{ product.featured_image.height }}"
                />
              {% endif %}
            </div>
            <div class="pcard__body">
              <h4>{{ product.title }}</h4>
              {% if product.type != blank %}
                <p class="scale">{{ product.type }}</p>
              {% endif %}
              <p class="price">{{ product.price | money }}</p>
            </div>
          </article>
          {% assign shown = shown | plus: 1 %}
        {% endfor %}
      {% endcapture %}
    {% endif %}

    {% if shown > 0 %}
      <div class="pgrid">{{ product_cards }}</div>
    {% else %}
      <div class="pgrid--empty">
        Select a collection in the theme editor to show products here.
      </div>
    {% endif %}

    {% comment %} Hardcoded DM Stash attribution lockup — see file header. Not a section setting. {% endcomment %}
    <div class="attrib reveal">
      <div class="attrib__unit">
        <span class="attrib__cap">Models by</span>
        <img src="{{ 'dm-stash.png' | asset_url }}" alt="DM Stash" />
      </div>
      <div class="attrib__div"></div>
      <div class="attrib__unit">
        <span class="attrib__cap">Printed by</span>
        <span class="attrib__name">Arcane Flame</span>
      </div>
      {% if section.settings.collection %}
        <a href="{{ section.settings.collection.url }}" class="svc__link" style="margin-left:auto">Browse the full range <span class="arw">→</span></a>
      {% endif %}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

